ting one token at a time. It compares its own probabilities for each position with the draft model\u2019s probabilities.\n\nThe key trick is that the verification step can accept or reject tokens in a way that preserves the target model\u2019s exact output distribution. For each proposed token, the algorithm accepts it if the target model assigns it at least as much probability as the draft model did. If the target model assigns lower probability, the token is rejected with probability equal to the difference, and a replacement token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences. This rejection-sampling rule ensures that the final sequence is distributed exactly as if the target model had decoded greedily or autoregressively on its own, while still benefiting from the draft\u2019s speed.\n\nIn practice, the speedup depends mainly on how many draft tokens are accepted before the first rejection, the relative cost of running the draft model versus the target model, and the batch size or hardware parallelism. If the draft model is too small or poorly aligned, it proposes tokens the target model rejects, wasting computation. If the draft is too long, verification costs rise and rejections become more likely.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft-target agreement, causing frequent rejections, and high verification or system overhead, especially when the target model is already efficiently batched or the draft model is not sufficiently cheaper.\n\nCount words. Let's count manually.\n", "top2": [[[1596, -0.005657613277435303], [1421, -5.928379535675049]], [[1144, -6.6757424974639434e-06], [513, -12.244610786437988]], [[4087, -0.24118953943252563], [310, -1.592889428138733]], [[1156, -0.0017670498928055167], [303, -6.4749250411987305]], [[579, -0.2108856588602066], [25, -2.0615994930267334]], [[1622, -0.0005994447274133563], [3296, -8.108769416809082]], [[25, -0.4761126935482025], [13, -1.02